window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][29] = "<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> A stated bar and severity rules turn an argument into a decision. <strong>When not to hold a release.</strong> For S3 issues and known low-risk defects; shipping with a known cosmetic flaw and a date to fix it beats delaying for perfection.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-c16-10-using-ai-tools-in-pm-work-what-counts-as-evidence\"></span>\n<h4>C16.10 Using AI tools in PM work: what counts as evidence</h4>\n<p>AI assistants can be used to draft specs, summarize research notes, generate test cases, review writing and analyze data. They change how fast a PM can produce text and analysis. They do not change what counts as evidence. FW-C16-08 sets the habits.</p>\n<p><strong>What they are good for.</strong> Drafting a first version from your bullet points; reformatting and editing; generating alternatives and counterarguments (a red team for your spec); listing edge cases and test cases; summarizing material you provide (interview notes, tickets) for you to check; explaining unfamiliar technical terms; writing queries or scripts that you then verify.</p>\n<p><strong>What they are not.</strong> A source of facts about your users or market. AI output is a draft or a hypothesis, not evidence: it may be wrong, may state invented numbers or sources with confidence, and may reflect biases in its training. Do not cite an AI answer as a source. If a number or claim matters, trace it to a primary source or to your own data.</p>\n<p><strong>Habits.</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Verify before you rely.</em> Check every fact, number, quote and citation against a source, especially anything that goes into a decision or a document others will rely on.</li>\n<li><em>Protect confidential data.</em> Know your company's policy; do not paste customer data, unreleased plans or personal information into tools that are not approved for it.</li>\n<li><em>Keep the decision human.</em> You own the judgment and the accountability; use the tool to widen the options, not to avoid choosing.</li>\n<li><em>Label AI-assisted work</em> where it matters (to colleagues, in documents) and never present synthesized text as user quotes.</li>\n<li><em>Watch for homogenized thinking.</em> A draft that sounds plausible can hide that it says nothing specific; add your own evidence and examples.</li>\n<li><em>Evidence from research stays from people.</em> An AI summary of interviews is a pointer to the transcripts; the transcripts and the participants are the evidence (C06). Synthetic &quot;users&quot; generated by a model are not users.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>In interviews.</strong> If asked, say how you use AI tools: for speed on drafts and structure, with verification and confidentiality habits, and with clear ownership of the decision. Interviewers are testing judgment, not tool names (C24 and C25 cover building with AI).</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> The habits capture the speed without the risk. <strong>When not to use them.</strong> When the data is confidential and the tool is not approved, or when the output cannot be verified at reasonable cost.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-3-frameworks\"></span>\n<h3>3. Frameworks</h3>\n<p>Eight frameworks, set out below. The decision memo (FW-C02-06), the success-metric template (FW-C05-06), the scope-negotiation script (FW-C15-07), the tradeoff triangle (FW-C15-05), the quality dial (FW-C10-05) and the experiment decision-rule table (FW-C14-05) are reused, not repeated.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c16-01\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C16-01 · The PRD skeleton · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a decided problem and goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a one- to three-page spec with eight sections: problem, goals and metrics, non-goals, users and stories, requirements, design and technical notes, risks and open questions, launch and measurement.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the team will read and discuss it; the scale of the document matches the stakes.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> writing costs time and saves rework; longer documents are read less.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a feature list; no non-goals; a problem statement that contains the solution.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 1.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sections: <strong>Problem · Goals and metrics · Non-goals · Users and stories · Requirements (must/should/could) · Design and technical notes · Risks, assumptions, open questions · Launch and measurement.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c16-02\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C16-02 · The good-versus-bad spec test · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a draft spec.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> answers to eight tests, a list of rewrites, and a verdict.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> an engineer and a tester can review it.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a review takes an hour and saves days.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> reviewing format and not content; accepting vague requirements.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the rewrites in Worked example 1.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Test</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Can a newcomer state the problem in a sentence, with no solution in it?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Is there a metric with a target and a guardrail?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Is there at least one non-goal a reasonable person might have expected in scope?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Can an engineer estimate each requirement?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Can a tester write a test for each requirement?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Are requirements prioritized?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Is the riskiest assumption named with a test?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Is it clear who decides what, and by when?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c16-03\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C16-03 · The user story and acceptance criteria · Established</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Origin:</strong> user stories arose in extreme programming practice (Kent Beck and colleagues) and were popularized by Mike Cohn (<em>User Stories Applied</em>, 2004); the INVEST checklist is by Bill Wake (2003) <code>[V: Beck with Andres, Extreme Programming Explained; Cohn 2004; Wake, INVEST, 2003]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a requirement from a user's point of view.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a story (&quot;As a... I want... so that...&quot;), acceptance criteria in given-when-then form, and an INVEST check.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the team shares an understanding of the user and the goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> stories keep the purpose in view and can fragment a coherent experience; keep the outcome alongside.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> stories as tasks; no acceptance criteria; stories too large to estimate; solution written into the story.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 2.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>INVEST: <strong>Independent · Negotiable · Valuable · Estimable · Small · Testable.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c16-04\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C16-04 · The estimate conversation · Original</h5>\n<p><strong>Core framework.</strong> Learn this one first.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a piece of work and an engineering lead.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a range with assumptions and confidence, the riskiest part, what would narrow the range, an outside-view check, and options that trade scope for date.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> engineers can estimate with some uncertainty; history of similar projects exists.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> ranges are less comfortable than dates and more honest.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> pressuring for a smaller number; treating the low end as a commitment; no checkpoints.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 3.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: <strong>Ask for a range and assumptions → Ask for the riskiest part → Check against similar past projects → Offer scope options for the date you need → Set checkpoints to re-estimate.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c16-05\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C16-05 · The technical-debt conversation · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a piece of debt, the team's capacity and the cost of working around it.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the principal (fix cost), the interest (cost per sprint), the payback period and net over a year, a sensitivity on the interest, and a recommendation compared with feature work.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the interest can be estimated from time lost, incidents or slowed delivery.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> paying down debt delays features; not paying slows all future work.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> treating debt as an engineering complaint; no numbers; fixing everything.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 4.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Formulas: <strong>payback = principal ÷ interest per sprint; net over N sprints = N × interest − principal; break-even interest = principal ÷ (N × capacity).</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c16-06\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C16-06 · The triangle conflict map · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a disagreement among product, design and engineering.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the tension named, each side's criteria, the cheapest evidence, and a decision owner and date.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the disagreement is about the problem and not personalities.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> evidence costs time; unresolved conflict costs more.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> escalating first; deciding by rank; hiding the disagreement.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> IQ-C16-04.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: <strong>Restate the shared goal → Name each side's criteria → Find the cheapest evidence → Decide (who, by when) → Commit and review.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c16-07\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C16-07 · The delivery rhythm · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a team and a goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a minimal set of practices (backlog in priority order, planning to capacity, short sync, demo, retrospective, definition of done, limit on work in progress) and the ones to drop.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the team can inspect and adapt.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> ceremony can replace judgment; too little structure hides problems.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> copying a process; meetings with no decisions; no definition of done.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 5 shows the definition of done at release.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"fw-c16-08\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C16-08 · The AI-assisted work check · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a task for which you would use an AI tool.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a decision to use or not, the habits applied (verify, protect data, own the decision, label, add evidence, keep research human) and a note of what remained unverified.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you know your organization's data and tool policy.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> speed against risk of unverified output and leaked data.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> citing AI as a source; pasting confidential data; presenting synthesized text as user quotes.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> IQ-C16-06.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>Habit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Will I rely on a fact or number?</td>\n<td>Verify against a primary source</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Is the data confidential?</td>\n<td>Use only approved tools; otherwise do not paste it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Whose judgment is this?</td>\n<td>Mine; the tool widens options</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Does it need labeling?</td>\n<td>Label AI-assisted work where it matters</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Is it user evidence?</td>\n<td>Only from people and transcripts</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c16-4-answer-architecture\"></span>\n<h3>4. Answer architecture</h3>\n<span id=\"c16-the-execution-answer-for-a-20-minute-round\"></span>\n<h4>The execution answer, for a 20-minute round</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Minutes</th>\n<th>What to produce</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Clarify scope and team</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>The goal, the team, the date, what is fixed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The spec</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Problem, goals and metrics, non-goals, requirements in priority order, risks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Partners and roles</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Engineering, design, data science and the other partners, and when each is involved</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Estimates and tradeoffs</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Ranges, assumptions, the outside view, scope options; technical debt if relevant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rhythm and quality bar</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Delivery practices that matter; definition of done; severity rules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Launch readiness and measurement</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Go or no-go criteria; how results will be read</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Pacing rules.</strong> If the interviewer asks about a specific problem (a slipping date, a conflict), spend the time on that and use the structure briefly. If you run behind, keep the spec outline and the way you handle the main friction.</p>\n<p><strong>How to say it.</strong> Be concrete: &quot;I'd write a two-page spec with the problem, a metric with a threshold, three non-goals and the requirements in priority order. I'd review it with the engineering lead and designer before anyone commits.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>How to close.</strong> Two sentences: &quot;I'd align the team on a short spec with explicit non-goals, treat estimates as ranges and negotiate scope, bring design, data science and the other partners in early, and hold a stated quality bar with a clear go or no-go rule. I'd change course if [signal].&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c16-type-walk-me-through-feature-to-launch\"></span>\n<h4>Type: walk me through feature to launch</h4>\n<p>Use the full structure; spend the most on the spec and the partners.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-type-engineering-pushback-or-a-slipping-date\"></span>\n<h4>Type: engineering pushback or a slipping date</h4>\n<p>Use the estimate conversation (FW-C16-04) and the tradeoff triangle (C15): ask for the range and the riskiest part, check against the outside view, offer scope options, set checkpoints.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-type-design-and-engineering-disagree\"></span>\n<h4>Type: design and engineering disagree</h4>\n<p>Use the conflict map (FW-C16-06): shared goal, criteria, cheapest evidence, decision owner.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-type-technical-debt-or-a-refactoring-request\"></span>\n<h4>Type: technical debt or a refactoring request</h4>\n<p>Use FW-C16-05: principal, interest, payback, sensitivity, and compare with features.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-type-a-release-decision\"></span>\n<h4>Type: a release decision</h4>\n<p>State the quality bar, triage by severity, count the facts and make the call with a rollback plan.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-type-using-ai-tools\"></span>\n<h4>Type: using AI tools</h4>\n<p>Say what you use them for, how you verify, how you protect data, and that the decision stays yours.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-common-interviewer-interventions-and-what-to-do\"></span>\n<h4>Common interviewer interventions, and what to do</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Interviewer says</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n<th>What to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What's in your spec?&quot;</td>\n<td>They want the content</td>\n<td>Walk the eight sections, with non-goals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What won't you build?&quot;</td>\n<td>Non-goals</td>\n<td>Give two non-goals a stakeholder might have expected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Engineering says it's six weeks.&quot;</td>\n<td>An estimate</td>\n<td>Ask for the range, the riskiest part and scope options</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Can't you just add people?&quot;</td>\n<td>Brooks's law</td>\n<td>Show the onboarding cost; offer scope instead</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;The designer disagrees.&quot;</td>\n<td>Conflict</td>\n<td>Restate the goal, name the criteria, find cheap evidence, decide</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;There are 40 open bugs. Ship?&quot;</td>\n<td>Quality bar</td>\n<td>Triage by severity; apply the release rule; count the risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Did AI write this?&quot;</td>\n<td>Judgment on tools</td>\n<td>Say how you use it, verify, and own the decision</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c16-5-worked-examples\"></span>\n<h3>5. Worked examples</h3>\n<p>All five examples are <code>Illustrative</code>: the products are fictional or use a product type as the setting only, and every figure is an invented assumption. The arithmetic was computed. Each is written as notes you could say aloud.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-worked-example-1-a-spec-for-admin-audit-logs-in-an-enterpris\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 1 — A spec for admin audit logs in an enterprise product (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: an enterprise collaboration product of the kind Microsoft offers. No fact about any real product is claimed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>The request.</strong> Large customers ask for &quot;audit logs.&quot; Security teams at two of them have said they need them for compliance reviews.</p>\n<p><strong>The problem statement, bad and good (FW-C16-02).</strong> <em>Bad:</em> &quot;Customers need audit logs.&quot; It names a solution. <em>Good:</em> &quot;IT administrators at organizations with compliance reviews cannot answer 'who changed this permission, and when' without opening a support ticket. Today a review requires an average of 6 support tickets per quarter per affected customer and delays audits by about 2 weeks (invented). We want administrators to answer these questions themselves, in under 5 minutes.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Goals and metrics (FW-C05-06).</strong> Primary: the share of eligible administrators who view the log at least once within 60 days of release (target 35%, invented). Input: median time to find a given event (target under 5 minutes). Guardrails: page performance (95th percentile load time must not rise by more than 2%), storage cost per customer within budget. Counter metric: support tickets about permission changes (should fall; if not, the log is not answering the question).</p>\n<p><strong>Non-goals.</strong> (1) Real-time alerts on events (a later release). (2) Customer-defined retention periods in version 1 (fixed at 90 days). (3) Export to third-party security tools in version 1. A reasonable stakeholder might have expected each of these, so naming them prevents arguments.</p>\n<p><strong>Requirements, in priority order.</strong> <em>Must:</em> record sign-ins, permission changes and file shares with who, what and when; search by person and date range; keep 90 days; show events within 5 minutes of occurrence. <em>Should:</em> export to a spreadsheet file. <em>Could:</em> saved filters.</p>\n<p><strong>Risks, assumptions, open questions.</strong> The riskiest assumption is that administrators will look at the log (a log nobody reads is cost without value). Test: interviews with ten administrators and a prototype shown to five, before the build. Other assumptions: the existing event stream carries the data needed (a technical spike will check). Open questions: retention rules by country (legal), storage cost (finance), how deleted users appear (design).</p>\n<p><strong>Partners.</strong> Legal and privacy for retention and personal data; security for access to the log itself (who may read it); support for training; engineering for the event stream; design for search and filters; data science for the instrumentation of log views.</p>\n<p><strong>Launch and measurement.</strong> Staged: ten design-partner customers, then general availability; go or no-go on the criteria in the spec (no S1 defects, search performance within target, support trained).</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The spec fits on two pages, separates the problem from the solution, names the non-goals and the riskiest assumption, and could be estimated and tested.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-worked-example-2-user-stories-and-acceptance-criteria-for-sh\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 2 — User stories and acceptance criteria for &quot;share a list&quot; (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>A weak story (invented).</strong> &quot;As a user, I want a share button.&quot; It states a control, not a purpose, so it cannot be tested beyond whether a button exists.</p>\n<p><strong>A better story (FW-C16-03).</strong> &quot;As a shopper planning a trip with a friend, I want to share my packing list so that my friend can see it and tick things off.&quot; It names who, what and why.</p>\n<p><strong>Too large.</strong> &quot;Manage who can see or edit my list&quot; is too big to estimate (it hides several behaviors). Split by workflow step into three stories, each valuable and testable on its own:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>View-only link.</em> Given I have a list, when I choose &quot;share&quot; and then &quot;view only,&quot; then a link is created that lets anyone with it see the list but not change it.</li>\n<li><em>Edit access for a named person.</em> Given I have shared a list, when I invite a person by email with edit access, then they can add and tick items and I see their changes within 10 seconds.</li>\n<li><em>Revoke access.</em> Given a person has access, when I remove them, then they can no longer open the list and see a message that access ended.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>INVEST check.</strong> Each story is independent (1 can ship without 2), negotiable (the how is open), valuable (a user sees a benefit), estimable (engineers can size each), small (a few days) and testable (the criteria are observable). The original &quot;manage who can see or edit&quot; failed small and estimable.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome, not only output.</strong> The stories deliver outputs. The outcome is more shared lists that two people use (for example, the share of shared lists with edits by both people within a week). The team should read that number after launch, and if the stories ship without it moving, ask why.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Splitting by workflow step and writing acceptance criteria turned a vague request into three testable increments.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-worked-example-3-the-estimate-conversation-that-s-eight-week\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 3 — The estimate conversation: &quot;that's eight weeks&quot; (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> The PM hopes to launch in 5 weeks. The engineering lead estimates 8 weeks for 10 stories (about 0.8 weeks each).</p>\n<p><strong>Step 1: ask for a range and assumptions (FW-C16-04).</strong> The lead says 5 to 12 weeks: 5 if an existing service can be reused, 12 if a new data model is needed, 8 as a middle view. The riskiest part is the data model. What would narrow it: a two-day technical spike.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2: the outside view.</strong> The team's last six similar projects took 1.1, 1.2, 1.3, 1.5, 1.6 and 2.0 times their estimates (invented): a median of 1.4 and a mean of 1.45. Applied to 8 weeks, that is 11.2 to 11.6 weeks. The honest planning range for the full scope is about 11 to 13 weeks, not 5.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3: do not negotiate the estimate; negotiate the scope.</strong> Options for a date:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Scope</th>\n<th>Estimate (8-week basis)</th>\n<th>With the outside view</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Full scope</td>\n<td>10 stories</td>\n<td>8 weeks</td>\n<td>about 11.6 weeks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Core scope</td>\n<td>6 stories</td>\n<td>4.8 weeks</td>\n<td>about 7.0 weeks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Smallest useful slice</td>\n<td>4 stories</td>\n<td>3.2 weeks</td>\n<td>about 4.6 weeks</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>To hit 5 weeks with confidence, the answer is option C: four stories, which deliver the core of the user's main job, and defer the rest to a second release. Option B fits 7 weeks.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4: set checkpoints.</strong> Do the two-day spike first. At the end of week 2, re-estimate with the new information. If the spike shows the existing service can be reused, the range moves toward 5 and more scope fits; if the data model is needed, the plan is already the smaller slice.</p>\n<p><strong>What I would say.</strong> &quot;I don't want to push the estimate down; I'd rather make the date true. With our history, the full scope is likely closer to 11 weeks. For 5 weeks, I'd ship the four-story core and add the rest in a follow-up. Let's run the spike this week and check again on Friday.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Pressure on the number would have produced a hidden overrun. The range, the outside view and the scope options produced a plan that could be met.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-worked-example-4-the-technical-debt-conversation-illustrativ\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 4 — The technical-debt conversation (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> An engineering lead asks for a full sprint to rework a fragile billing module. The PM must weigh it against features.</p>\n<p><strong>The numbers (FW-C16-05) (invented).</strong> The team has 4 engineers in 2-week sprints: 8 person-weeks per sprint. The lead estimates that workarounds, bug fixes and slow changes around the module cost about 15% of capacity, 1.2 person-weeks per sprint (the interest). The rework costs 6 person-weeks (the principal). Payback: 6 ÷ 1.2 = 5 sprints, or 10 weeks. Over six months (13 two-week sprints), the rework saves 13 × 1.2 = 15.6 person-weeks and costs 6, a net gain of 9.6 person-weeks, which is more than a sprint's worth of feature work.</p>\n<p><strong>Sensitivity.</strong> The interest is the uncertain number. If it is only 8% of capacity (0.64 person-weeks per sprint), payback is 9.4 sprints and the net over six months is 13 × 0.64 − 6 = +2.3. The rework pays within six months as long as the interest exceeds about 5.8% of capacity (6 ÷ 13 ÷ 8). Ask the team to measure it: how many tickets, incidents and hours trace to the module over the last quarter?</p>\n<p><strong>Beyond capacity.</strong> Debt also carries risk: this module handles billing, so an outage or error costs money and trust. That belongs in the case alongside the time cost (a defect rate and its cost, for example).</p>\n<p><strong>Compare it with features (C15.5).</strong> If the best alternative use of 6 person-weeks returns, say, $90,000 in expected value, and the rework frees 15.6 person-weeks over six months (which could return about $234,000 at the same rate), the rework is worth more.</p>\n<p><strong>The decision.</strong> Do the rework, but not as a full sprint of nothing else: split it into two stages of three person-weeks each, with a measure of the interest after the first stage (hours lost on the module). If the measured interest is under the break-even, stop after stage one. Tell the sales and support teams what the sprint will and will not deliver, and the date features resume.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The conversation became an investment case with a payback, a sensitivity and a way to check it, instead of a request that sounded like a complaint.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-worked-example-5-a-release-decision-40-open-bugs-illustrativ\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 5 — A release decision: 40 open bugs (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> Eight working days before launch, QA reports 40 open defects (invented): 3 S1, 9 S2, 28 S3. Two engineers are available for fixes: 16 person-days.</p>\n<p><strong>The release rule, set in advance (C16.9).</strong> No open S1. S2 allowed only with a documented workaround and an owner. S3 backlogged.</p>\n<p><strong>Triage.</strong> Of the 9 S2 defects, 5 are on the core path (checkout) and 4 are in secondary screens with workarounds. Estimates: each S1 takes about 2 days, each core-path S2 about 1.5 days.</p>\n<p><strong>The arithmetic.</strong> S1: 3 × 2 = 6 person-days. Core-path S2: 5 × 1.5 = 7.5. Total 13.5 of 16 available, leaving 2.5 person-days (16%) as a buffer. The 4 secondary S2 defects ship with workarounds, and the 28 S3 defects go to the backlog.</p>\n<p><strong>Sensitivity.</strong> If the S1 fixes take 4 days each instead of 2, the total is 3 × 4 + 7.5 = 19.5, which exceeds 16. Then something must give: delay by about two days, or fix only the S1s and the three most severe core-path S2s, or launch to a smaller group first. Re-estimate after the first S1 is fixed (day 2): if the first takes 4 days, the rest are likely similar, and the decision moves up.</p>\n<p><strong>The decision and the safety net.</strong> &quot;Fix the three S1s and the five core-path S2s, ship with the four secondary S2s and 28 S3s documented, and launch in stages: 10% of users for two days with guardrails on checkout completion and errors, then 50%, then all. Rollback is tested before day one. Support has the workarounds. Go or no-go meeting on day 7 with the severity counts and the staged results.&quot; Accessibility and security checks are release criteria, not defects to trade.</p>\n<p><strong>What I'd tell the stakeholders.</strong> &quot;We will ship on the date with no S1 and the core path clean. Four secondary issues have workarounds and are scheduled for the first patch. If the first S1 fix takes longer than expected, I will tell you by day 2, and the choice will be a two-day delay or a smaller first release.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A rule set in advance, severity triage, capacity arithmetic and a staged launch turned a long bug list into a decision.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-6-baseline-strong-standout\"></span>\n<h3>6. Baseline, Strong, Standout</h3>\n<p><strong>Prompt:</strong> &quot;Outline the spec for a 'saved filters' feature on a search page, and say what you would not include.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c16-baseline-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Baseline answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;I'd write down the features: a button to save a filter, a list of saved filters, and a way to apply or delete them. I'd also add notifications when new results match and sharing of filters with others.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>What is wrong:</em> a feature list with no problem, user or metric. The &quot;not include&quot; part is missing (everything that came to mind went in). It cannot be estimated or tested.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-strong-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Strong answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;I'd start with the problem: people who run the same search repeatedly, such as a buyer checking daily for new listings, have to set the same filters every time. I'd state the goal with a metric: for returning searchers, cut the time to reach their results from about 40 seconds to under 10, and raise the share who return within a week. Guardrail: page performance. Non-goals: notifications about new matches (a later release), sharing filters with other people, and syncing across accounts.</p>\n<p>&quot;Requirements in priority order: must save the current filters with a name, list and apply saved filters in one tap, rename and delete; should keep the saved filters across devices; could let people reorder them. Each requirement has acceptance criteria that QA can test. I'd name the riskiest assumption (that people repeat searches enough to use saved filters) and check it with search logs before building.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd review the spec with the engineering lead and designer before committing, and ask for an estimate as a range.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c16-standout-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Standout answer</h4>\n<p>Everything in the Strong answer, plus:</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd decide how large the spec needs to be: for a small feature, two pages with the problem, metrics, non-goals and stories. I'd put the decision log at the top so that settled questions stay settled.</p>\n<p>&quot;On non-goals, I'd choose ones people would plausibly expect: notifications are the likely next request, and saying so now avoids scope creep. I'd also say that the first version stores filters on the device only, if that halves the work, and that cross-device sync follows if usage justifies it, which makes the minimum viable version the test of the riskiest assumption.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd involve privacy early, because saved searches can reveal what a person is looking for, and they may be stored; I'd confirm where they live and how long. I'd ask data science to define how we will tell that the feature changed behavior, not just that people used it: the share of saved-filter users who return and the time to results compared with similar users without it, with a holdout if possible.</p>\n<p>&quot;The quality bar: accessible controls, an empty state that explains what saved filters are, and an error state if saving fails. Release criteria: no S1 defects, search performance within 2% of today, rollback tested.</p>\n<p>&quot;What would change my mind: if search logs show that fewer than 5% of users repeat a search, I'd drop the feature or fold it into a lighter 'recent searches' list.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c16-what-moves-an-answer-up-a-band\"></span>\n<h4>What moves an answer up a band</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>From Baseline to Strong</th>\n<th>From Strong to Standout</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>States the problem, a metric and non-goals</td>\n<td>Chooses non-goals by what stakeholders would expect and sizes the first version to test the riskiest assumption</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Writes testable requirements in priority order</td>\n<td>Involves privacy, data science and quality criteria early</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reviews with engineering and design before committing</td>\n<td>Names what would make you drop or shrink the feature</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c16-7-company-lens\"></span>\n<h3>7. Company lens</h3>\n<p>All claims describe what companies say publicly or what candidates commonly report, not insider knowledge. Interview formats change <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Microsoft [as of 2026-10].</strong> Microsoft's products serve enterprise customers whose administrators, security teams and compliance officers are partners in what ships <code>Inferred</code> <code>[unverified: author's reasoning from public product descriptions; no source checked]</code>; the company publishes inclusive-design material <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Microsoft Inclusive Design toolkit manual]</code>. Practical consequence: in a spec, name the administrator and the compliance requirements as users, involve security and legal early, and say how you would stage a rollout to design-partner customers.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Google [as of 2026-10].</strong> Google has published its approach to reliability and postmortems <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Google SRE book on postmortems]</code>, and has described a culture of design documents reviewed by engineers before major work (not checked here) <code>Inferred</code>. Candidates commonly report execution questions probing how you work with engineers at scale <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: expect to be asked how you would invite technical review, how you treat estimates, and how you decide go or no-go.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Amazon [as of 2026-10].</strong> Amazon has described writing a future press release and FAQ before building, narrative documents in place of slides, and a principle of disagree and commit <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Amazon working-backwards material; 2017 shareholder letter; Leadership Principles]</code>. Practical consequence: be ready to write the customer's announcement and the hardest questions it would draw, and to describe a time you disagreed and committed.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Meta [as of 2026-10].</strong> Meta has publicly changed its engineering motto over time, from moving fast and breaking things to moving fast with stable infrastructure, which shows the tension between speed and quality <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Fortune, 16 February 2022, on Meta's values and the 2014 motto change]</code>. Candidates commonly report questions about balancing launch speed with quality and about working with engineers on tight timelines <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: state your quality bar and your staged-launch practice.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Apple [as of 2026-10].</strong> Apple integrates hardware, software and services, and third-party guides report that execution prompts concern cross-functional work in which design and engineering craft are given great weight <code>Inferred</code> <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. Practical consequence: show respect for craft, describe how you protect quality when schedules press, and name how you handle dependencies across hardware and software.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Netflix [as of 2026-10].</strong> Netflix's published culture material describes high-trust, high-accountability teams in which informed individuals decide <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Netflix culture memo, June 2024]</code>. Practical consequence: describe who decides, on what context, and how you give engineers context instead of instructions.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Stripe [as of 2026-10].</strong> Stripe is widely described as having a strong writing culture and as emphasizing API design and documentation quality <code>Inferred</code> <code>[V: The Pragmatic Engineer, Inside Stripe's Engineering Culture Part 2 (strong culture of writing); Slab interview with Stripe's former Documentation Manager]</code>. Practical consequence: write specs that engineers and developers could use as documentation, with explicit contracts, error behavior and non-goals.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c16-8-common-mistakes\"></span>\n<h3>8. Common mistakes</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Mistake</th>\n<th>How to detect it</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>A spec that is a feature list</td>\n<td>No problem, metric or non-goals</td>\n<td>Use the PRD skeleton; start with the problem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>A problem statement that contains the solution</td>\n<td>&quot;Users need a better X&quot;</td>\n<td>Rewrite with who, what happens and the cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>No non-goals</td>\n<td>Scope arguments keep returning</td>\n<td>Name two things a stakeholder might expect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Untestable requirements</td>\n<td>&quot;Fast,&quot; &quot;easy,&quot; &quot;intuitive&quot;</td>\n<td>State a measurable threshold and a condition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Pressing for a smaller estimate</td>\n<td>The number shrinks and the date slips</td>\n<td>Ask for a range; negotiate scope</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Adding people late</td>\n<td>&quot;We'll add engineers&quot;</td>\n<td>Do the onboarding arithmetic; cut scope</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Treating technical debt as a complaint</td>\n<td>&quot;Not now&quot; with no numbers</td>\n<td>Principal, interest, payback; compare with features</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Design as decoration</td>\n<td>Designers brought in after the spec</td>\n<td>Include design in discovery; critique against the problem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Late partners</td>\n<td>Legal or security hears two weeks before launch</td>\n<td>Use the partner table; involve them at design</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>No release rule</td>\n<td>The go or no-go is argued bug by bug</td>\n<td>Set severity rules in advance; stage the launch</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c16-9-mental-models-applied\"></span>\n<h3>9. Mental models applied</h3>\n<p>Four models, each tied to a decision in this chapter. The fence idea is from C08.</p>\n<p><strong>Conway's law</strong> (<code>MM-conway</code>). Organizations tend to produce systems that mirror their communication structure <code>[V: Conway, 1968, as quoted in several sources]</code>. If three teams must agree to ship a feature, the feature will have three seams. Use it to shape the work: put the people who must make decisions together, and expect the architecture to follow the team boundaries, for better or worse.</p>\n<p><strong>Hofstadter's law</strong> (<code>MM-hofstadter</code>). It always takes longer than you expect, even when you take into account Hofstadter's law. The point is practical: add margin, use the outside view (C15), and re-estimate at checkpoints, since confident early estimates are the least reliable.</p>\n<p><strong>The fence, applied to code</strong> (<code>MM-chestertons-fence</code>, C08). Before removing or rewriting something that looks like clutter, learn why it exists. A strange rule in a billing module may encode a legal requirement or a fix for an old failure. The technical-debt conversation should start with understanding, not contempt.</p>\n<p><strong>Make the change easy, then make the easy change</strong> (<code>MM-easy-change</code>). A phrase attributed to Kent Beck <code>[V: Kent Beck, Software Design: Tidy First? newsletter, 30 January 2025, repeats the line; also quoted in Paranj, Test Driven Development in Ruby, 2017; date of first appearance not checked]</code>: when a feature is hard to add, first restructure so that it becomes easy, then add it. For a PM, it justifies sequencing a small refactoring before a feature when the feature would otherwise be built on a weak base.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-engineering-design-and-data-science-perspectives\"></span>\n<h3>Engineering, design and data-science perspectives</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Engineering.</strong> Engineers want a clear goal, early involvement, stable priorities and respect for estimates. They can tell you which parts are risky and where the cheaper route is. Bring them into problem framing and listen for &quot;that's hard because...&quot;, which is often the start of a better design.</li>\n<li><strong>Design and research.</strong> Designers want to understand the problem and the user, not just receive a list. Research wants to feed the spec, not audit it. Share the goal and constraints early, and ask for options.</li>\n<li><strong>Data science.</strong> Data scientists want clarity on the decision, the definition of success and what will be logged. Involve them before the metrics section is final, and again before launch to check instrumentation.</li>\n<li><strong>Legal, security, support and operations.</strong> They hold constraints and the cost of late surprises. Give them a short brief with what is changing and when, and ask what they need to see by when.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c16-checklist-before-you-start-building\"></span>\n<h3>Checklist: before you start building</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Can the problem be stated in one sentence with no solution in it?</li>\n<li>Is there a metric with a target, a guardrail and a counter metric?</li>\n<li>Are there non-goals a reasonable person might have expected in scope?</li>\n<li>Is each requirement estimable and testable, in priority order?</li>\n<li>Did engineering give a range, with assumptions, and did I check it against history?</li>\n<li>Is the riskiest assumption named with a cheap test?</li>\n<li>Have legal, security, support, design and data science seen the plan at the right time?</li>\n<li>Are the quality bar, severity rules and go or no-go owner set, with a rollback plan?</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c16-10-case-studies\"></span>\n<h3>10. Case studies</h3>\n<span id=\"cs-c16-01\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C16-01 · The spec that said &quot;improve search&quot; · Illustrative · Beginner</h5>\n<p><em>Fictional composite. Every number is invented (invented).</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> An e-commerce team of four engineers (24 person-weeks over three sprints) is told by a stakeholder, in one line of a ticket, to &quot;improve search.&quot; The PM adds nothing to the ticket. Engineers read it as a quality project and build type-ahead suggestions.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> A fixed team, a quarter-end review, and no written problem statement or metric.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> After the team ships type-ahead at the end of sprint three, the search metrics that the stakeholder cares about barely move: 18% of queries still return no results, and 60% of the people who see no results leave without buying. Interviews with the stakeholder reveal the real goal: cut zero-result queries, which were losing sales. Of the 24 person-weeks, 9 (37.5%) went into type-ahead, which did not address the goal.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> As an observable problem: &quot;The team built what the words suggested, not what the stakeholder needed, because the ticket stated neither the problem nor the goal.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> The spec failed the good-versus-bad test (FW-C16-02) on every count: no problem sentence, no metric, no non-goals, no testable requirements. Engineers filled the gap with a reasonable interpretation, and nobody could tell it was wrong until after the build. The cost of a clarifying conversation at the start, about two hours with the stakeholder, was far smaller than 9 person-weeks of rework. Once the goal was stated, the solution turned out to be small: handle common misspellings and synonyms so that fewer queries return nothing, about 4 person-weeks.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Keep the ticket and iterate on what shipped</td>\n<td>No rework conversation</td>\n<td>The goal stays unmet</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Write a two-page spec with the problem, a metric, non-goals and requirements, then rebuild the scope</td>\n<td>The actual goal</td>\n<td>A conversation; discarded work</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Ask the stakeholder to specify the solution</td>\n<td>Speed</td>\n<td>A solution chosen by someone not close to the system</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B costs a short meeting and gives the team something to estimate and test. C hands the design to the wrong person. A repeats the problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Choose B. The spec: <em>Problem:</em> 18% of searches return no results and 60% of those users leave. <em>Goal:</em> cut zero-result queries from 18% to under 8%, without lowering relevance (guardrail: click-through on results). <em>Non-goals:</em> no new ranking model, no type-ahead changes this quarter. <em>Requirements:</em> spelling correction for the top misspellings, a synonym list for the top 200 terms, and a &quot;no results&quot; page that suggests related searches. <em>Primary metric:</em> the zero-result rate.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> In this fictional scenario the zero-result rate fell from 18% to 7% (invented), delivered in 4 person-weeks, and click-through on results did not change. The type-ahead work stayed shipped as a modest improvement. The PM added a two-page spec template to the team's process.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> If the zero-result rate had fallen only to 12%, the team would have examined the remaining queries (rare products, long queries) and set a second goal. If the stakeholder had wanted something else again, the spec would have exposed the disagreement in the first meeting and not after a quarter. The lesson: the cheapest place to fix a project is the first page of the spec.</p>\n<span id=\"cs-c16-02\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C16-02 · HealthCare.gov, 2013: launching without stable requirements or verified performance · Verified · Advanced</h5>\n<p><em>Sources (types): the US Government Accountability Office report GAO-14-694, &quot;Healthcare.gov: Ineffective Planning and Oversight Practices Underscore the Need for Improved Contract Management&quot; (31 July 2014), read through GAO's highlights and page extracts, and the GAO testimony of 31 July 2014 (GAO-14-824T), read in full (primary); GAO's September 2014 report on security and privacy controls (GAO-14-730), read through its highlights; and news coverage of the report (CNBC, FEDweek, KFF Health News, Fierce Healthcare). The analysis is the author's. This case concerns a government program built by contractors; it is used here for what it shows about specs, decision rights and release criteria, and it makes no comment on policy. Re-read in October 2026.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> The Centers for Medicare &amp; Medicaid Services (CMS) contracted in September 2011 for the systems behind the federal health insurance marketplace, accessed through Healthcare.gov. Enrollment opened on 1 October 2013, a date set by the program's legal timeline. When it opened, many users experienced website failures, errors and slow responses. GAO's review found that CMS had developed Healthcare.gov and its related systems without effective planning or oversight practices, and that costs rose and schedules slipped, primarily because requirements kept changing, a problem made worse by inconsistent oversight.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> The program was a first-of-its-kind federal marketplace, a complex effort under a fixed launch date. The work was divided among multiple contractors, under a cost-plus-fixed-fee arrangement that GAO described as high risk for the government, while saying it may have been a reasonable choice in the circumstances <code>[V: GAO-14-824T]</code>. The date could not move easily: it was tied to the enrollment period.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As reported by GAO: CMS issued task orders for the federally facilitated marketplace (FFM) and the data hub when key technical requirements were still unknown, including the number and composition of states to be supported and the number of potential enrollees, and proceeded with the award process before requirements were stable; CMS officials said that meeting deadlines drove several planning decisions, including the choice of a cost-plus-fixed-fee contract; CMS did not develop a required acquisition strategy; from September 2011 to February 2014 estimated FFM development costs rose from an initial obligation of $56 million to more than $209 million, and data hub costs from $30 million to almost $85 million, due primarily to changing requirements exacerbated by inconsistent oversight; CMS moved an assessment of FFM readiness from March to September 2013, weeks before launch, and did not receive required governance approvals, so it launched &quot;without verification that it met performance requirements&quot;; because of unclear roles, GAO found about 40 instances in which program staff inappropriately authorized contractors to spend funds, totaling over $30 million; CMS gave the FFM contractor written concerns in April and November 2013, moved program staff to the contractor's offices in September 2013, and ultimately declined to pay about $267,000 in requested fee; and in January 2014 CMS awarded Accenture Federal Services a contract estimated at $91 million to continue FFM development, whose costs had risen to over $175 million by 5 June 2014 with key capabilities, including the financial management module, still unavailable <code>[V: GAO-14-824T; GAO-14-694 highlights]</code>. CMS reported obligating $840 million for the development of Healthcare.gov and its supporting systems as of March 2014 <code>[V: GAO-14-824T]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> <em>The author's reading.</em> The project had a fixed date, unstable requirements, divided accountability and a late and reduced test of whether the system worked. As an observable problem: &quot;The system was launched to the public on a fixed date without verification that it met its performance requirements.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> <em>The author's analysis.</em> Read through this chapter's tools, several of its themes appear at scale. <em>Specs:</em> work was ordered when key requirements were unknown and requirements kept changing; a stable statement of the problem, the goals and the non-goals (FW-C16-01) is the antidote to churn, and the report links the cost and schedule growth to changing requirements. <em>The triangle:</em> with the date fixed (by law), scope and quality were the levers; the evidence suggests quality verification was what gave (FW-C15-05). <em>Decision rights:</em> confusion about who could decide is a failure of the roles and decision ownership that C16.5 and C32 describe. <em>Quality bar and release criteria:</em> a go or no-go rule with stated performance criteria, and a check that they were met, would have exposed the gap; the readiness review that was moved later is the very mechanism that should have caught it (C16.9). <em>Launch approach:</em> a full public launch left no room to learn at small scale; a staged rollout (C17) can limit exposure when a date cannot move, though it may not have been possible within the legal constraints <code>[inference]</code>. <em>Partners:</em> security and privacy were later the subject of a separate GAO report (GAO-14-730, September 2014), which found that Healthcare.gov had weaknesses when first deployed, including incomplete security plans and privacy documentation, incomplete security tests and no alternate processing site <code>[V: GAO-14-730 highlights]</code>, a reminder that partners and verification belong in the plan early.</p>\n<p>The case does not show that any individual made a wrong choice, and it does not show what a different approach would have achieved. It shows what the public record says about the conditions: a hard date, unstable scope, split accountability, and a verification step that was late and incomplete.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong> <em>The author's reconstruction of choices a program team could have faced; not a record of CMS's deliberations.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Launch on the date with the full scope, as happened</td>\n<td>Meeting the date</td>\n<td>Unverified performance; failures at the public launch</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Delay the launch until performance is verified</td>\n<td>Quality</td>\n<td>Missing a legally set date; consequences for the program</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Launch on the date with a reduced scope or limited exposure, with verified performance on the core path</td>\n<td>Meeting the date and protecting the core</td>\n<td>Fewer features or users at first; political and legal constraints may have limited this</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D. Freeze requirements early and test integrated performance well before launch</td>\n<td>Stability and evidence</td>\n<td>Earlier decisions; an earlier hard conversation about scope</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A met the date and carried the quality risk. B protected quality and broke the date. C is the middle path and the one the triangle favors when the date is fixed; D is the cheapest in the long run and the hardest to do politically, because it forces scope decisions early.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> As documented, the system launched on 1 October 2013 without verification that it met performance requirements.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> Reported: many users could not complete enrollment at launch; costs and schedule grew; GAO recommended better contract management and governance. How the system later improved, and the effect of recovery efforts, are outside the sources used here.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> <em>Inference.</em> A frozen requirements baseline and early integrated testing would have surfaced the performance problems when there was time to fix them. A limited launch might have reduced the number of people affected by early failures. A reader should be careful with hindsight: the same facts would be read differently if the launch had worked. The method to carry away: write the problem and non-goals so that requirements stop moving, set the release criteria in advance and verify them, make decision rights explicit, bring partners in early, and when the date is fixed, reduce scope or exposure before you reduce verification.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-11-interview-questions-and-model-answers\"></span>\n<h3>11. Interview questions and model answers</h3>\n<p>Seven questions: two Beginner, two Intermediate, three Hard. Model answers are <code>Illustrative</code> (composite candidates), every figure is an assumption, and the arithmetic was computed. Each is written against the six behaviors in section 1. Where a real company's product is the setting, the answer reasons from public information and states no internal fact. The answers are fuller than a first pass in a real round.</p>\n<span id=\"iq-c16-01\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C16-01 · Beginner · execution · EX</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> What goes into a good PRD?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The requirements and the designs.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Lists the sections (problem, goals, non-goals, requirements, metrics, risks) and says why each exists.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Distinguishes good from bad examples, scales length to stakes, treats the document as a living tool for alignment, and mentions the decision log and the partners.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> A PRD exists to align a team on what to build and why, so the best test of one is whether someone new could read it and explain the problem, the goal and what is out of scope.</p>\n<p><em>Problem.</em> Who has what problem, with evidence, and the cost of leaving it. No solution in it: &quot;Shoppers who search by name get no results on 18% of queries, and 60% leave&quot; and not &quot;we need a better search.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Goals and metrics.</em> The outcome and how we will measure it: a primary metric with a threshold, a guardrail, and a counter metric (C05).</p>\n<p><em>Non-goals.</em> Things a reasonable stakeholder might have expected that we are not doing in this version. They prevent scope creep and end arguments early.</p>\n<p><em>Users and stories.</em> Who is affected, and the key scenarios as short stories with acceptance criteria that QA can test.</p>\n<p><em>Requirements.</em> In priority order (must, should, could), each specific enough that an engineer can estimate it and a tester can test it: &quot;results load in under 1.5 seconds at the 95th percentile,&quot; not &quot;fast.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Risks, assumptions and open questions.</em> The riskiest assumption and the cheap test for it, what could go wrong, and what is undecided with an owner and a date.</p>\n<p><em>Launch and measurement.</em> How it ships (staged), who decides go or no-go and on what criteria, how results will be read.</p>\n<p>I'd scale it to the stakes: two pages for a small feature, longer with a summary on top for a multi-team launch. I'd keep a decision log at the top and update the document as we learn, and I'd review it with the engineering lead and designer before anyone commits. The mistake is writing a long document that no one reads or a feature list with no problem. Those are signals that the spec is for the author, not the team.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How long should a PRD be, and who should read it? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;As long as the stakes require and no longer. A small change may need one page: the problem with evidence, the goal and metric, non-goals and acceptance criteria. A large cross-team launch may need ten pages plus links to research, designs and a decision log. The test is that an engineer, a designer and a stakeholder can each find what they need in a few minutes, and that someone new could explain the problem and what is out of scope. I would share it early as a draft for comment, because a PRD that was written alone and handed over arrives too late to be corrected.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you keep the PRD useful after it is written? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By treating it as a living tool. Keep a decision log at the top (date, decision, reason, who decided), update acceptance criteria when scope changes with the tradeoff stated, and link it from the tickets so that it is the place to look. Review it at the start of each sprint with engineering and design, and retire sections that no longer apply. A PRD that is not updated becomes fiction, and a team that stops reading it falls back on memory and meetings.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c16-02\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C16-02 · Beginner · execution · EX</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> You expected a feature to take four weeks. Engineering says twelve. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Push back and ask them to find ways to be faster,&quot; or &quot;accept it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks why, gets a range and assumptions, and looks at scope options.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses the outside view, separates complexity from risk, proposes a smaller first slice and checkpoints, and avoids pressure on the estimate.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> A threefold gap between what I expected and what engineering says is information. Either my expectation was missing something about the system, or the scope is bigger than I realized. I'd start with curiosity, not pressure.</p>\n<p><em>Understand.</em> I'd ask for the breakdown: where does the time go, what is the riskiest part, what assumptions does 12 weeks rest on, and what is the range? Suppose it is 8 to 16 weeks, driven by a data model that does not exist yet. Then I'd learn that my four-week picture assumed reuse of something that is not there.</p>\n<p><em>Check my side.</em> What did I assume, and from what? If I had a reason (a similar feature took four weeks), compare with this one: what differs?</p>\n<p><em>Use the outside view.</em> How have this team's estimates compared with actuals? If their past projects ran 1.1 to 2.0 times their estimates, 12 weeks may itself be optimistic, which means planning for four is not realistic for the full scope.</p>\n<p><em>Negotiate scope, not the number.</em> If I need something in four to six weeks, ask: &quot;What is the smallest version that delivers the core of the user's job, and how long does it take?&quot; Perhaps a first slice that handles the common case in 4 to 5 weeks, with the full version later. Or a two-day technical spike to narrow the range before we commit.</p>\n<p><em>Decide and communicate.</em> Option 1: ship the smaller slice in five weeks and the remainder in a follow-up. Option 2: keep full scope and plan 12 to 16 weeks, with a checkpoint at week 3. I'd explain the tradeoff to stakeholders with the range and the reason. What I would not do is ask engineers to promise four weeks for the full scope. That produces hidden shortcuts and a late surprise.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you use the outside view on the estimate? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I ask how long comparable work took. If similar features in this system took on average 1.5 times the first estimate, a 12-week estimate may run to 18, and I would plan with a range, not a point. I would also ask engineering what the riskiest unknown is and whether a short spike (two to three days) could shrink the range. The outside view is not a way to pressure the estimate down; it is a way to avoid planning on the hope that this time nothing will go wrong.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What smaller first slice would you propose, and how would you check in? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I would ask which part delivers most of the user value with the least of the complexity, perhaps the core flow for one platform or one segment, and aim for a version in four to six weeks. Checkpoints at weeks two and four show progress against the range: if the riskiest part is on track at week two, confidence rises; if not, we re-scope early. I would agree with engineering what is cut from the first slice and when the rest would follow, and tell stakeholders the new plan with the reasons.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c16-03\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C16-03 · Intermediate · execution · EX · Microsoft</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> You are shipping admin audit logs in an enterprise product. Legal wants 180-day retention, security wants the logs encrypted and access-controlled, finance is worried about storage cost, and support wants training. How do you coordinate?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Hold a meeting with everyone and get alignment.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies what each partner needs and when, and sequences the work.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Puts numbers on the cost tradeoff, spots a conflict between requirements (retention versus erasure requests), proposes options and a decision owner, and plans a staged rollout with design partners.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I know the product only from public information; nothing here describes any real plan. I'd treat each partner as holding a constraint that is cheap to meet early and expensive late, and sequence accordingly.</p>\n<p><em>Legal and privacy first.</em> Retention length and the handling of personal data shape the design. They may also create a conflict: logs kept 180 days for audit may contain personal data that a person can ask to have erased in some jurisdictions <code>[V: GDPR Article 17 right to erasure, unless retention is legally required, as summarized in guidance]</code>. I'd ask legal for the rule, not guess, and put the answer in the spec (for example, logs record identifiers that can be pseudonymized on request, with the audit trail intact).</p>\n<p><em>Security.</em> Design review of how logs are written (tamper-resistant), who can read them (administrators only, with their own access logged), and encryption. Early involvement avoids a high-risk finding in the week before launch.</p>\n<p><em>Finance.</em> Put numbers on retention. Suppose (invented) storage costs $4 per customer per month at 90 days and $8 at 180 days; for 2,000 customers that is $8,000 against $16,000 a month, $96,000 a year more. Options to put to finance and product leadership: include 90 days in all plans and sell 180 days as a higher tier; include 180 for all and absorb the cost; or tier by customer size. I'd bring the options with the cost and the demand evidence (how many customers need 180 days), not a request.</p>\n<p><em>Support and operations.</em> Training, scripts for the questions they will receive (&quot;why can't I see events older than 90 days?&quot;), and an escalation route. Involve them two to three weeks before launch.</p>\n<p><em>Engineering and design.</em> Confirm the event stream carries the data; design search and filters.</p>\n<p><em>Sequence and ownership.</em> Week 1: legal, security and finance decisions with a named owner each. Weeks 2 to 6: build. Week 5: support training. Launch: staged, first to ten design-partner customers, with go or no-go criteria in the spec (no S1 defects, search within the performance target, support trained). I'd keep a decision log and a short weekly note to all partners.</p>\n<p><em>Where I'd decide.</em> If legal and finance conflict, I'd bring the options to the shared leader with a recommendation. What would change my plan: legal saying that a particular market needs a different retention rule, which would turn retention into a regional setting.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Microsoft is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that enterprise-product prompts involve compliance and security partners <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Microsoft data or product behavior is claimed.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you handle the conflict between 180-day retention and erasure requests? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By asking legal for the rule and designing so that both can be met. A common approach is to pseudonymize personal data in the logs: store tokens in place of names and emails, and keep the mapping in a separate protected store that can be deleted when a person asks to be erased, so that the audit record stays intact while the personal link is removed. Legal and privacy decide which obligation takes precedence for which data <code>[V: GDPR Article 17 right to erasure, unless retention is legally required, as summarized in guidance]</code>, and I would record the decision and its owner. The design should not make either requirement impossible later.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you put numbers on the storage cost and stage the rollout? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Suppose 5 million events a day at about 1 KB each: 5 GB a day, 900 GB for 180 days. With three copies and indexing that might multiply the volume fivefold, about 4.5 TB, which at an assumed $0.023 per GB-month is roughly $100 a month. So cost is small, and the real costs are engineering time for access control and encryption and support training. I would stage the rollout with the ten design-partner customers for four weeks, test retrieval and export, then release to a small share of customers before general availability, with a guardrail on query latency and access-control defects.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c16-04\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C16-04 · Intermediate · execution · EX</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Your engineering lead and your designer both think your plan is wrong, for different reasons. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Overrule them&quot; or &quot;do what they say.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Listens to each, restates the shared goal, and looks for evidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates the criteria each is using, finds the cheapest test, names the decision owner, commits afterward, and keeps the relationships intact.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> When two of my closest partners disagree with my plan, I'd take it as a strong signal and not as an obstacle. I'd talk to each separately first, then together.</p>\n<p><em>Listen for the criteria.</em> The engineer thinks it is too risky or too big: the criterion is cost and reliability. The designer thinks the experience will confuse users: the criterion is user value and usability. They may be seeing different parts of the same flaw. I'd ask each: &quot;What would you do instead, and what are you worried will happen if we go with my plan?&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Restate the shared goal.</em> &quot;We all want [goal] by [date]. Do we agree on that?&quot; If there is disagreement about the goal itself, that is the real conflict and I'd resolve it first.</p>\n<p><em>Find the cheapest evidence.</em> Is there a way to test the contested points quickly? A paper prototype tested with five users answers the designer's concern in two days; a technical spike answers the engineer's in two or three. I'd propose both and agree beforehand what result would change the plan.</p>\n<p><em>Decide and own it.</em> If the evidence is clear, change the plan and say what I learned. If it is not, I decide, since the call on scope and priority is mine, and I explain the reasoning and the risk I am accepting, and invite them to say what would make us revisit. Then we commit: &quot;disagree and commit,&quot; with a date to review.</p>\n<p><em>Protect the relationship.</em> I'd thank them for the challenge, credit their input in the final plan, and make sure that raising concerns early is rewarded. What I'd avoid is deciding by rank, hiding the disagreement or reopening the decision every week. What would change my mind: any evidence that the plan fails the user or cannot be built in time.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What is the cheapest test for each concern? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;For the designer's concern, a clickable prototype with five users, which costs two or three days and shows whether the experience confuses people. For the engineer's concern, a technical spike on the riskiest part, a day or two of building a rough version to find out how hard it is. Both are cheap compared with a quarter of building, and both turn opinions into evidence. I would agree with each of them beforehand what result would change my plan, so that the tests are real and not a formality.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you commit afterward and keep the relationships intact? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Name who decides before the test. After the evidence, the decision owner decides, I say what I learned from each of them, and everyone commits, with a record of the dissent and the signal that would reopen the decision. I would credit the engineer and designer in front of others for the changes that came from their objections. Partners who feel heard even when overruled stay willing to disagree openly next time, which is what a team needs.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c16-05\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C16-05 · Hard · execution · EX</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Your engineers ask for a full quarter to refactor a core system. Sales wants three features. How do you decide?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Features, because they drive revenue,&quot; or &quot;refactor, because debt is dangerous.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks for the cost of the debt and compares it with the features.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes payback and the effect of risk, shows sensitivity, proposes staging with a measure, and communicates the tradeoff to sales.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'd refuse to treat it as engineering against sales and ask the same question of both: what does each return for the capacity it uses? For the refactoring I need the principal, the interest, and the risk.</p>\n<p><em>Numbers (invented).</em> Six engineers, two-week sprints: 12 person-weeks a sprint. The refactor costs 24 person-weeks (two sprints, the principal). Workarounds cost about 10% of capacity, 1.2 person-weeks per sprint (the interest). Payback on capacity alone: 24 ÷ 1.2 = 20 sprints, about 40 weeks. Over a year (26 sprints), capacity savings are 26 × 1.2 = 31.2 person-weeks, more than the 24 spent: a net of +7.2 person-weeks on time savings alone, and payback takes the first 20 sprints of the year.</p>\n<p><em>But debt also carries risk.</em> Suppose the system has two serious incidents a year, each costing $150,000 in lost revenue and recovery, and the refactor would roughly halve them: $150,000 a year of avoided loss. At about $15,000 per person-week of engineering value (invented), the time savings are 31.2 × $15,000 = $468,000, plus $150,000 of avoided incidents, $618,000 a year, against a cost of 24 × $15,000 = $360,000. So the year-one return is about +$258,000, and over two years it is $1,236,000 against $360,000, a gain of $876,000. The case is positive in year one and strong over two, if the interest and the incident rate hold.</p>\n<p><em>Compare with the features.</em> Size the three sales features the same way (C15.5): expected value per person-week. If they return more than $15,000 per person-week, they win this year; if less, the refactor is at least as good.</p>\n<p><em>Sensitivity.</em> The interest and the incident rate are the uncertain numbers. If interest is 5% rather than 10%, the time savings halve to $234,000 and the year-one return is about break-even (+$24,000), and if the incident saving also disappears it is −$126,000. So I'd ask the team to measure the interest: hours lost to the system over the past quarter, and the incident history.</p>\n<p><em>Decision.</em> Not a whole quarter of nothing else. Stage it: do the highest-interest part first, perhaps 8 person-weeks, with a measure of hours saved and incidents after, and ship the most valuable one or two sales features in the same quarter. Tell sales what they get and when, and why the refactor is part of reliability they rely on. Review at the end of the first stage. What would change my mind: a major incident in the system, which would move the whole refactor up, or a sales feature tied to a signed commitment.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> The refactor pays back in about 20 sprints. How do you decide? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;On the full picture, not payback alone. The principal is 24 person-weeks and the interest is about 1.2 person-weeks per sprint of workarounds, so payback on workaround time alone is about 20 sprints, around nine months, which is long. Then add risk: if the old system has a 5% chance per quarter of an outage that costs $400,000, the expected loss is about $20,000 a quarter. I would propose a staged slice: refactor the module that causes most of the workarounds in six weeks, measure the drop in workaround time and incidents, and decide on the rest with evidence.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you communicate the tradeoff to sales? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Directly and with dates. I would say: this quarter we deliver two of the three features and a six-week slice of the refactor, because the system's instability puts the revenue at risk and slows every later feature; the third feature follows next quarter. I would show the revenue each feature is tied to and what the refactor protects, and ask which feature matters most to open deals. Sales needs a clear answer and a date they can give customers, and a promise I cannot keep is worse than a later date.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c16-06\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C16-06 · Hard · execution · EX</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> A teammate suggests using an AI tool to write the PRD and summarize the user research for you. What do you think, and what is your approach?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Great, it saves time,&quot; or &quot;I wouldn't use it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names what it is good for and what it is not, with verification.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Distinguishes drafts from evidence, protects confidential data, keeps the decision and the research with people, labels the work, and gives a concrete workflow.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> <code>[as of 2026-10]</code> I'd use it for speed on drafts and structure, and I'd be strict about what counts as evidence. A model's output is a draft or a hypothesis. It is not a source.</p>\n<p><em>For the PRD.</em> I'd write the problem, the goal and the non-goals myself, because they are my judgment, and give the tool my bullet points to turn into a first draft, to suggest missing sections, to list edge cases and to argue against the plan (a red team). Then I'd edit hard: check every number, every claim about users and every technical statement against a source, and remove generic text that says nothing specific.</p>\n<p><em>For the research.</em> The evidence is the participants and the transcripts (C06). I could use a tool to organize notes I give it, to propose themes and to find quotes, but I'd check each theme against the transcripts and quote only what a person actually said. A summary is a pointer to the transcripts, not a replacement for them. And a model-generated &quot;user&quot; is not a user: its answers say what text on the internet looks like, not what my customers do.</p>\n<p><em>Data and privacy.</em> I'd follow the company's policy: no customer data, unreleased plans or personal information in tools that are not approved for them. For approved tools, I'd still strip identifiers when I can.</p>\n<p><em>Ownership and honesty.</em> The decision, and the accountability, stay with me and the team. I'd say in the document where AI helped and not present synthesized text as findings. If the PRD contains a claim I cannot trace to a source or my own data, it comes out.</p>\n";
